6-75. When Giulia went around town in problem 6-50 comparing the prices of pizzas, she also gathered data on the prices of garlic knots. Because garlic knots come in different sizes, she decided to collect data on the weight of each serving, in grams. Giulia’s father approximates that he will make garlic knot servings that will weigh around 288 grams each.

Restaurant / Weight per Serving (g) / Price ($):
Paolo’s Pizza / 155 / 4.89
Nick’s Cafe / 310 / 8.00
The Red Door / 320 / 7.49
Roman Oven / 258 / 6.50
JohnBoy’s Pizza Delivery / 186 / 6.00
Lily’s / 168 / 4.50
Rosa’s Restaurant / 352 / 8.69
Charles F. Cheese / 252 / 7.00

With your team, create a detailed analysis of the relationship between the weight of the garlic knots and their price that includes but is not limited to:
• a scatter plot of the data with appropriately labeled axes;
• the equation of the LSRL with defined variables;
• an interpretation of both the y-intercept and the slope;
• interpretation of positive and negative residuals;
• upper and lower bound lines drawn;
• a statement explaining whether there is a causation between the variables;
• a description of the association, including numerical and graphical justifications for all conclusions; and
• a suggestion for reasonable prices that Giulia’s father could charge for his garlic knots.

7-40. Graph your data for “The Penny Lab” carefully on the sheet protector of the Lesson 7.1.5 Resource Page.
a. How can you describe the scatter plot representing all teams’ Penny Lab data? Consider:
• where the graph crosses the y-axis;
• whether the graph is linear or curved;
• whether there are any asymptotes;
• whether the graph is continuous or discrete;
• the end behavior;
• whether the graph is increasing or decreasing; and
• the percent rate of change.
b. Write an equation for a function that models the data. Make sure you also write a “let” statement for your variables.
c. What output does your function give for x = 0? What could this mean in relation to the situation?
d. Could there be an output value for x = −1? If so, what might it mean?
e. What could be the domain of the model that you wrote in part (b)? With this domain, what would a graph of your model look like?
f. What family of functions that you saw in previous chapters has graphs like the one you made in part (e)? What are the first few values of this function? What is the equation?
